🟢 TOPIC 2: CLASSIFICATION EVALUATION METRICS
1. Why Do We Need Evaluation Metrics?

After training a classification model, we need to check how well it predicts unseen data.

For example:

A model predicts whether patients have a disease.

Simply knowing that it achieves 90% accuracy may not be enough.

We also need to know:

How many sick patients were correctly identified?
How many healthy patients were incorrectly flagged?
How many sick patients were missed?

These questions are answered using classification evaluation metrics.

2. Confusion Matrix

A confusion matrix compares actual classes with predicted classes.

There are four outcomes:

Term	Actual	Predicted	MeaningTP	1	1	Correct positive
TN	0	0	Correct negative
FP	0	1	Incorrect positive
FN	1	0	Incorrect negative
Example

Suppose:

TP = 40
TN = 45
FP = 5
FN = 10

Confusion matrix:

Actual / Predicted	Positive	NegativePositive	TP = 40	FN = 10
Negative	FP = 5	TN = 45

Total observations:

40+45+5+10=10040+45+5+10=100
Memory Trick
True = Correct
False = Incorrect
Positive = Predicted class 1
Negative = Predicted class 0
3. Accuracy

Accuracy measures overall correctness.

Formula
Accuracy=TP+TNTP+TN+FP+FN\boxed{Accuracy=\frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}}
Example
Accuracy=40+45100Accuracy=\frac{40+45}{100} Accuracy=85%\boxed{Accuracy=85\%}
Meaning

85 out of 100 predictions were correct.

Important

Accuracy can be misleading when the dataset is imbalanced.

Example:

990 healthy patients
10 sick patients

A model predicting everyone as healthy achieves 99% accuracy but identifies no sick patients.

4. Precision

Precision measures how many predicted positives are actually positive.

Formula
Precision=TPTP+FP\boxed{Precision=\frac{TP}{TP+FP}}
Example
Precision=4040+5Precision=\frac{40}{40+5} Precision=88.89%\boxed{Precision=88.89\%}
Meaning

Out of 45 patients predicted to have the disease, 40 actually have it.

When Is Precision Important?

When False Positives are costly.

Example:

Spam detection.

An important email incorrectly classified as spam is a false positive.

Memory Trick: Precision = How trustworthy are positive predictions?

5. Recall

Recall measures how many actual positives were successfully detected.

Also called:

Sensitivity
True Positive Rate (TPR)
Formula
Recall=TPTP+FN\boxed{Recall=\frac{TP}{TP+FN}}
Example
Recall=4040+10Recall=\frac{40}{40+10} Recall=80%\boxed{Recall=80\%}
Meaning

Out of 50 patients who actually have the disease, 40 were identified.

When Is Recall Important?

When False Negatives are costly.

Examples:

Disease screening
Fraud detection
Safety-critical detection

Memory Trick: Recall = How many actual positives did we find?

6. F1-Score

F1-Score combines Precision and Recall using their harmonic mean.

Formula
F1=2×Precision×RecallPrecision+Recall\boxed{F1=2\times\frac{Precision\times Recall}{Precision+Recall}}

Alternative formula:

F1=2TP2TP+FP+FN\boxed{F1=\frac{2TP}{2TP+FP+FN}}
Example
F1=2(40)2(40)+5+10F1=\frac{2(40)}{2(40)+5+10} =8095=\frac{80}{95} F1=84.21%\boxed{F1=84.21\%}
When Is F1 Useful?

When:

Precision and Recall both matter.
Classes are imbalanced.
You want one score combining both metrics.

F1 does not directly include True Negatives.

7. Important Additional Metrics
Specificity

Measures how many actual negatives were correctly identified.

Specificity=TNTN+FP\boxed{Specificity=\frac{TN}{TN+FP}}

Example:

Specificity=4545+5Specificity=\frac{45}{45+5} Specificity=90%\boxed{Specificity=90\%}
False Positive Rate
FPR=FPFP+TN\boxed{FPR=\frac{FP}{FP+TN}}

It measures the proportion of actual negatives incorrectly classified as positive.

ROC-AUC

ROC stands for Receiver Operating Characteristic.

The ROC curve plots:

True Positive Rate
False Positive Rate

across classification thresholds.

AUC summarizes the area under the ROC curve.

Higher ROC-AUC generally indicates better ranking ability.

For binary classification, an ROC-AUC of 0.5 corresponds to chance-level ranking.

8. Complete Formula Table
Metric	Formula	MeaningAccuracy	(TP + TN) / Total	Overall correctness
Precision	TP / (TP + FP)	Correctness of predicted positives
Recall	TP / (TP + FN)	Detection of actual positives
F1	2PR / (P + R)	Balance of precision and recall
Specificity	TN / (TN + FP)	Detection of actual negatives
FPR	FP / (FP + TN)	Incorrect positive rate
